      "hints": [
        "Integrate $f(x) = 2(x + 2)^{-2}$ to get $-2(x + 2)^{-1}$.",
        "Evaluate limits from $1$ to $\\infty$ to get $0 - \\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = \\frac{2}{3}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Integrate $f(x) = 2(x + 2)^{-2}$ with respect to $x$:\n$$\\int 2(x + 2)^{-2} dx = \\frac{2(x + 2)^{-1}}{-1} = -\\frac{2}{x + 2}$$",
        "Evaluate the improper definite integral over the limits $[1, \\infty)$:\n$$\\int_1^\\infty f(x)\\,dx = \\left[ -\\frac{2}{x + 2} \\right]_1^\\infty = \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(-\\frac{2}{x + 2}\\right) - \\left(-\\frac{2}{1 + 2}\\right) = 0 - \\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = \\frac{2}{3}$$"
      ],
